.TH factor "" "Command"
.SH NAME
factor \- print the prime factors of a number
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBfactor [\fInumber ...\fB]\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
factor -- print the prime factors of a number
Usage:	factor [ number ... ]
If no 'number' is given, numbers are read one per line from
the standard input until end of file, a zero, or a non-numeric
character is seen.
.HE
.B factor
prints, on a single output line, the prime factors of each
.I number
given on the command line, separated by spaces, in increasing order,
each repeated as many times as it divides the number.
.PP
If no
.I number
is given,
.B factor
instead reads numbers from the standard input, one at a time,
skipping leading white space, and factoring each in turn.
It stops reading as soon as it sees end of file, the number
.BR 0 ,
or a character that is not part of a number.
.PP
A
.I number
may be written in decimal, or, with a leading
.BR 0 ,
in octal, or, with a leading
.B 0x
(letters
.B a
through
.B f
allowed, in either case), in hexadecimal.
Numbers are held internally in floating point, so very large values
may be factored, subject to the precision of a
.B double
and to the time needed to search for factors by trial division.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.B expr
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.B factor
prints
.B "Number too big"
and exits if an input number has too many digits, and
.B "bad number"
or
.B "Number too big"
(with the offending text) if a command-line argument is not a valid
number or overflows.
